The {{nihongo|'''Asahi Line'''|朝日線|Asahi-sen}} is a subway line in [[Okami|Okami (大紙)]], [[Okaiken]], operated by [[Okami Metro]]. The line functions as a "J-shaped" distributor, connecting the northwestern commercial districts of [[Fukushima Ward, Okami|Fukushima]] and [[Nakanoshima]] to the southern hub of [[Tennoji]], running primarily underneath the bustling Sakaisuji Avenue. On system maps, the line is color-coded golden yellow ({{Okami Metro color|A}}), and its stations use the prefix "A".

While shorter than the arterial [[Miyoshi Line]] and [[Tozai Line (Okami)|Tozai Line]], the Asahi Line is critical for relieving congestion in the eastern half of the city center. It connects the "Cultural Corridor" of [[Nakanoshima]] with the electronics and pop-culture district of [[Kuromon-Bashi]].



==History==
Construction of the Asahi Line was approved in the 1960s as part of the "City Center Relief Plan." The first segment, connecting the business district of [[Yodoyabashi-Oike Station|Yodoyabashi]] to the cultural hub of [[Kuromon-Bashi Station|Kuromon-Bashi]], opened on April 15, 1965.

The line was extended northward to [[Noda-Hanshin Station|Noda-Hanshin]] in 1969 to connect with the [[Hanshi Electric Railway]], and southward to [[Tennoji-Koen Station|Tennoji-Koen]] in 1974 to intercept commuters from the southern suburbs. The final extension to [[Fumin-nosato Station|Fumin-nosato]] was completed in 1980.

Unlike the Miyoshi and Tozai lines, which were built with 10-car platforms, the Asahi Line was designed for 6-car trains, reflecting its role as a distributor rather than a primary trunk line. However, frequency is high, with trains running every 3 minutes during peak hours.

